<p>Jason Torchinsky at The Autopian joins me in shock at the revelation that <a href="https://www.theautopian.com/a-typography-geek-pointed-out-something-about-the-space-shuttle-discovery-and-now-i-cant-unsee-it/">the Helvetica S on the space shuttle Discovery is upside-down</a>:</p>
<blockquote><p>
  Wait, what? <em>Discovery</em> has an upside-down ‘s’ in its name? How the hell did that happen? <em>Discovery</em> was usually considered, by astronauts and NASA people, the jewel in the Space Shuttle crown. It was recognized to be the most reliable and best-performing of all the orbiters, and <a href="https://airandspace.si.edu/collection-objects/orbiter-space-shuttle-ov-103-discovery/nasm_A20120325000">had the most missions and time in space</a>. Since it was first launched in 1984, <em>Discovery</em> completed 39 missions, spent in total a full year in space, and traveled 150 million miles.</p>
<p>  The thing flew for 27 years, and somehow nobody noticed that the ‘s’ was upside down on the starboard side name text?
</p></blockquote>
